WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.969
It’s hard to believe it’s been a full
one hundred days since I began my journey

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:08.309
– no, my mission – into the beating heart
of the SCP Foundation.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:14.589
I, Stathis Stern, risked detection, my safety
and even my own life for this cause, and I’m

00:00:14.589 --> 00:00:19.180
sure that makes you wonder why on Earth I’d
be foolhardy enough to put so much on the

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.180
line.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.580
Well, my reason is the best – no, the only
– reason that matters: for the sake of art.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.939
I decided early on that recording my findings
was essential, both for posterity and so that

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:37.020
the art world may know just how dedicated
I have been in my pursuit of true inspiration.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.551
It all started over three months ago, that
was when I first managed to embed myself in

00:00:41.551 --> 00:00:43.270
the SCP Foundation.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.120
Naturally, I never would have jumped at the
idea of having to fraternize with the likes

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.200
of the Chaos Insurgency.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.359
Their methods are far too brutal, their organization
seeped in violence that trivializes my own

00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:56.530
pursuit of a deeper meaning.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.530
But even I have to admit, the Insurgency’s
methodology can yield results.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.111
They arranged for a convenient ‘accident’
to befall a lower-level researcher, after

00:01:06.111 --> 00:01:12.730
they returned home from Site-19; the SCP Foundation’s
largest and most infamous facility, home to

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.450
many of their equally-infamous anomalies.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.930
Thanks to a falsified identity and phony transfer
order from the Insurgency, I was able to slip

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.610
into Site-19 undetected, so that I could begin
my search.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.740
The subterfuge was, at first, easy to maintain.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.150
I looked the part, obviously.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:35.460
A lab coat and name badge, so versatile and
yet completely unassuming.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.570
I was visible and still imperceptible to the
rest of the Foundation’s personnel; as far

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.270
as they knew, I was just the latest transfer
to Site-19.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:49.320
As I spent the day getting my bearings, not
one of them could have possibly known my true

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.560
intention.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.210
My goal was simple and twofold.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:57.820
First and foremost: I had long felt I deserved
to be accepted as a member of the art collective

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:59.340
known as ‘AWCY?’

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.549
or ‘Are We Cool Yet?’.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:06.479
Since the nineteenth century, they have been
championing the creation and exhibition of

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:12.170
anomalous artwork, truly pushing the boundaries
of what can be considered artistic expression

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.470
despite the Foundation’s attempts to censor
AWCY’s transcendent art.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.340
I had admired their mission statement from
afar, for a number of years, and strived to

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.660
achieve much the same in my own artwork.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.220
However, none of my previous pieces quite
met the collective’s anomalous pedigree.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.470
I know a way to change that, though…

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:36.379
Blending in at Site-19 proved to be fairly
simple; as long as you stride around with

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:38.920
purpose, few will question what you’re doing.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.940
With my head in a notebook, or a look of conviction
on my face, I could freely make my way around

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.080
the facility.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.700
But why, you might wonder?

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.120
What was the actual intention behind my undercover
mission?

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.800
Well, the answer lies in the second half of
my goal: recognition.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.410
Not just notoriety and acceptance into the
ranks of AWCY, but fame throughout the anomalous

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.760
art world – and all the perks that could
come with.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.780
Of course, the main hurdle to overcome was
I needed to create a piece of art that was

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.099
anomalous in its nature.

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:15.500
And so, rather than wait for inspiration and
materials to fall into my lap, I chose to

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.269
instead seek out what I needed.

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:22.750
The anomalies on offer at Site-19 were abundant,
and knowing where to start was tricky.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.909
I made the decision, while still getting accustomed
to my new surroundings, that I would start

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:30.390
wherever I felt the most drawn to.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:36.720
Before the end of my first week in Site-19,
I felt the first pull of an anomaly, and uncovered

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.879
it in the most unlikely of places.

00:03:39.879 --> 00:03:44.599
During the day, most of the Foundation living
quarters are empty, meaning I could sneak

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:46.610
around there undetected.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:53.290
It was in the personal bathroom of one Foundation
doctor that I found SCP-063.

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.290
To the untrained eye, the anomaly appears
to be an ordinary toothbrush, save for the

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:02.140
wording stenciled into its pale blue plastic:
“The World’s Best Tothbrush”.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.040
Of course, examining it closer, I learned
of its ability to cleave through inorganic

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.900
matter like a hot knife through butter.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:13.360
It was quite the shock, slicing the porcelain
of the bathroom’s sink by accident.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.980
That would have led to my detection, had I
not left soon after.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.199
But I reflected on the toothbrush – or ‘Tothbrush’
– and what it could possibly represent.

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:26.940
Perhaps an acknowledgment of rituals, and
their importance to human health.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:32.030
Yet, at the same time, being sharp enough
to cut anything not organic – anything not

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:38.400
human – maybe SCP-063 was meant to symbolize
the possible dangers of habitual behaviors.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.400
The next day, I was able to bear witness to
an anomalous piece I had only ever heard rumors

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.400
of.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.699
For a time, I had thought it to have been
the reason for AWCY’s founding; after all

00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:52.360
its nickname was derived from a particular
traditional art form.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.680
SCP-173, The Sculpture, as those at the Foundation
call it.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:02.300
I was able to insert myself into a group of
two other personnel; a researcher and security

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.300
officer.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.650
The former was entering the container housing
SCP-173 to run tests, the latter protecting

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:09.650
her.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:13.509
Naturally, I overheard them looking for a
third and offered my assistance, even if all

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:16.310
I had to do was watch The Sculpture intently.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.949
I wasn’t even allowed to blink without getting
someone else to cover for me while I did.

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:22.680
I adored it.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.870
The messy structure of concrete and rebar
defied all notions of what many believed art

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:32.160
should be; it wasn’t beautiful or pristine,
it dared to be ugly.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:38.430
And yet, it demanded to be seen, to be looked
at and perceived, or else it would break my

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.539
neck and claim my life.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:44.850
It was everything I’d hoped: pure art, personified.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:50.319
It was as I neared the end of my first week
in Site-19 that I discovered my next anomalous

00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:54.010
art piece, hidden away in a secure storage
locker.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:57.259
SCP-010, the Collars of Control.

00:05:57.259 --> 00:06:01.199
These were a collection of cast iron collars
that, according to their file, could be used

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:06.680
to control the actions and movements of a
person wearing one, via a remote control.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:08.830
These collars fascinated me.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:13.819
They were, to my interpretation, tools to
control yet simultaneously a rejection of

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:16.880
the very rules that control human beings.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.539
Someone wearing an SCP-010 collar could potentially
have their entire bodily shape reconfigured

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:24.650
by the person wielding the remote.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:29.349
That alone, the fact these objects could be
used to defy the physical laws that dictate

00:06:29.349 --> 00:06:35.190
the forms we exist in, was a meta comment,
rejecting the laws of society in order for

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.270
free expression.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:42.940
Expression only achieved by art; it all made
me appreciate AWCY’s dedication to anomalous

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:43.940
artworks.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.680
Yet, I was still determined to be welcomed
into their collective.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:52.810
My first week hiding within Site-19 concluded
with a visit to SCP-107.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:57.319
Held on a pedestal in an otherwise empty room
was an empty, hollowed-out turtle shell.

00:06:57.319 --> 00:07:01.340
Now, I, for one, don’t respect archaeologists.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:06.210
Art historians are another matter; however,
I would rather focus on translating the human

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:11.379
experience into my art than waste mental energy
on events or objects from hundreds and thousands

00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:12.919
of years ago.

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:16.639
But this turtle shell might be the exception.

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:21.379
Apparently, any liquid placed within the shell
will be absorbed, only to rain down from the

00:07:21.379 --> 00:07:22.539
clouds above.

00:07:22.539 --> 00:07:27.680
There’s something quite honest about SCP-107;
interpreted through the mind of an artist

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:33.080
such as myself, it appears to be a reminder
of the natural world’s cyclical process.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:37.390
The liquid within it becomes rain, which falls
and feeds the plants.

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:43.270
The plant life in turn provides food and shelter
for animals, humans too, and so on.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:48.490
On and on, the circle of life repeats, as
so embodied by the hollow shell of an ancient,

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:49.490
proud turtle.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:54.009
I began my second week of subterfuge with
a scare, a close call that I may have been

00:07:54.009 --> 00:07:55.009
discovered.

00:07:55.009 --> 00:07:59.580
One of the Foundation doctor’s had complained
that his sink had been sliced in two, in spite

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.110
of my best efforts to cover my tracks.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:07.610
It seemed that using his denture glue to repair
the damage SCP-063 had done didn’t quite

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.870
have the effect I had intended.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:14.449
Nevertheless, the reaction by the Foundation
seemed to imply that that sort of thing happened

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:15.800
all the time.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:20.539
An announcement was made reminding all researchers
not to toy with The World’s Best Tothbrush,

00:08:20.539 --> 00:08:24.139
“despite how fun it was to slice things
up with”.

00:08:24.139 --> 00:08:28.479
Although they requested the culprit come forward,
I decided to divert the Foundation’s suspicions

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:33.399
away from me, hinting to a security officer
I had seen Doctor Alto Clef leaving the private

00:08:33.399 --> 00:08:36.560
bathroom where I had accidentally broken the
sink.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.620
They seemed to believe he had been capable
of causing the damage.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.130
Almost being discovered meant I had to reassess
my objective.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:49.160
Over the following few days, I tried to sink
into life among the Foundation’s researchers,

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:54.680
playing the role of a helpful member of personnel
so as not to attract any further suspicions.

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.589
And so, I was mostly concerned with the general
day-to-day tending to anomalies.

00:08:59.589 --> 00:09:03.950
Most of them I encountered during this time
were living creatures, which were of little

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.079
interest to me.

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:10.660
Although one, by the designation of SCP-049,
sported the rather aesthetically striking

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:14.330
appearance of a Plague Doctor, complete with
dark robes and a beaked mask.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:18.560
At first, I thought him to be quite an amicable,
intelligent fellow.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:23.190
That is, until I had a moment alone with him
to ask his opinions on art.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:28.200
The Plague Doctor claimed it was ‘of little
importance’ when compared to curing ‘the

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.200
pestilence.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:33.570
Naturally, I took that to be a personal insult,
requesting to the Research Head that I be

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:38.050
transferred away from an anomaly that was
obviously not as intelligent as I first thought.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:43.510
Two and a half weeks had passed, and I had
still remained undetected by the Foundation.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:46.490
However, I had been forced to reassess my
mission.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:51.300
AWCY were hardly likely to accept me into
their group if I had nothing to show for my

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:52.300
efforts.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:57.180
I needed either some materials I could use
to create an anomalous art piece or – preferably

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.300
– something I could claim as my own.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:04.610
It wasn’t as if the upper echelons of AWCY
possessed their own detailed files on every

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:06.750
anomaly at Site-19.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:11.920
They would have no idea if something I plucked
from containment was an existing SCP or a

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.320
Stathis Stern original piece.

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:20.920
So, I set about finding something that could
be my Magnum Opus, my breakthrough into the

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:26.910
anomalous art community – my key to the
fame, fortune, and artistic acclaim I so obviously

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:27.910
deserved.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:32.680
You may call it plagiarism, using another
anomaly as my own piece, but nobody technically

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:36.040
owns the objects and entities at Site-19.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:41.000
Even the Foundation themselves are more jailors,
and curators, than creators.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:46.620
I, however, am a visionary – I just needed
something to prove it.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:51.329
My search took me far and wide, all over the
width and breadth of Site-19.

00:10:51.329 --> 00:10:55.430
By the time my first whole month had passed,
I had begun to walk its corridors and pass

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:58.690
by its containment chambers just like any
other researcher.

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:02.510
Although, not one who I interacted with had
any clue they were in the presence of the

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:05.410
man who would reshape the anomalous art world.

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:10.930
There was an SCP transferred from another
facility that I witnessed during that time.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:16.350
According to the Foundation’s files, SCP-066
had previously been a ball of braided yarn

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:21.310
and ribbon, but had since become an amorphous
ball of meat with functioning eyes.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:25.910
What struck me the most about SCP-066 was
the question it asked since transforming.

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:27.060
“Are you Eric?”

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.589
At first, I thought, I’m not Eric.

00:11:29.589 --> 00:11:35.360
But the longer I pondered it, I began to wonder,
allowing my perception to be challenged.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.630
That is what art does, after all.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:42.139
Perhaps, much in the Marshal Mathers sense
that ‘We are all Stan’, all of us may

00:11:42.139 --> 00:11:44.100
well be Eric too…

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:50.340
I can recall another incident encountering
some SCP-131s, creatures known as the Eye

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.899
Pods, when one almost tripped me up.

00:11:52.899 --> 00:11:57.720
Their brightly-colored teardrop-shaped bodies
were certainly striking, a rather abstract,

00:11:57.720 --> 00:11:59.950
modernist biomechanical design.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:04.320
But it was their eyes that I found myself
ruminating on.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:10.540
SCP-131 instances had a single, unblinking
eye, giving a constant gaze without faltering.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:16.880
I likened it much to the uncritical, unthinking
eyes of certain audiences, who in the past

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.529
had started at my artwork and had nothing
interesting to say about it.

00:12:20.529 --> 00:12:25.910
In other events, my attempts to avoid detection
may have proven a little detrimental.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.480
Doctor Clef cornered me, a little perturbed
that I had implicated him in the ‘sink slicing’

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.480
incident.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:36.560
He at one point he seemed to imply a desire
to inflict far worse damage with… a ukulele

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:37.560
of all things.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.519
Although, I’m sure he was just joking.

00:12:39.519 --> 00:12:41.240
At least, I hope so.

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:46.560
The whole interaction reminded me of the urgency
of my mission, I still needed my anomalous

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:51.010
masterpiece, and would need to have an escape
plan in place once I found it.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:55.360
During my next stint of time hidden among
the ranks of the Foundation, I tried to broaden

00:12:55.360 --> 00:13:00.110
my own interpretation of what AWCY would consider
to be ‘art’.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:04.240
Nothing in their admittedly vaguely defined
rules stated my anomalous piece couldn’t

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.380
be created from a more modern medium.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:12.570
Never mind paintings that can depict a person’s
death or a sculpture that moves whenever unobserved,

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:16.670
what about something like photography, I thought
to myself.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:21.370
However, it was around this time that my hopes
for such a piece were seemingly answered,

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:27.690
in the form of SCP-105, or Iris Thompson as
she introduced herself.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:32.000
Iris had a rather unique relationship with
the art of photography.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:36.820
She possessed an ability to alter images taken
on a specific Polaroid Express camera from

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:38.560
1982.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:44.920
In the hands of SCP-105, the photographs would
come to life, like slices of real-time frozen

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.410
one moment then moving the second she held
them.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:54.420
She could even alter these images, reaching
into the Polaroid to move or retrieve objects.

00:13:54.420 --> 00:14:00.329
It instilled an idea in me, perhaps taking
a polaroid of my chosen anomalous art, would

00:14:00.329 --> 00:14:01.430
be enough.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:05.790
That is, until I realized the photographs
were only interactable to Iris, and breaking

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:09.430
her out of containment was a waste of my skills
as an artist.

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:13.750
Partway into my third month hiding out in
Site-19, part of me began to wonder if the

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:18.000
Foundation’s security forces were as formidable
as I’d been led to believe.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.149
It seemed I barely even needed to hide my
mission to join AWCY, or that I had spent

00:14:23.149 --> 00:14:25.519
so much time in their facility undetected.

00:14:25.519 --> 00:14:30.329
It was on my sixty-eighth day that I decided
to reflect on some of the potential anomalous

00:14:30.329 --> 00:14:33.040
pieces I had encountered so far.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:37.560
Frustratingly so, I was still yet to discover
any that would wipe the smug grins off the

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:41.840
AWCY members, only to replace them with looks
of awe.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:47.360
Something in Site-19 would guarantee my spot
among the anomalous art collective, I was

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.360
certain of it.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.560
I just had to find it.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:55.120
A potential candidate presented itself, one
that might have even allowed me to get my

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:59.190
own back on AWCY for denying my entry for
so long.

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:06.610
I was shown SCP-3037, a miniature model that
depicted the city of Dubrovnik in Croatia.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:10.459
I was watching it and pondering the meaning
behind this sculpture, thinking about why

00:15:10.459 --> 00:15:14.070
it had been made specifically to look like
Dubrovnik, of all places.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:19.610
Maybe, I pondered, it was meant to be a commentary
or condemnation of urbanization, given the

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:24.990
city’s walls keeping its residents contained,
confined, much like how we as humans have

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:29.750
become trapped by our urban settings, finding
comfort in being confined there.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:36.709
However, it was while examining SCP-3037 that
I apparently suffered something of an incident.

00:15:36.709 --> 00:15:42.240
From the moment I touched it, I spoke in only
Serbo-Croatian, believing myself to actually

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:44.850
be the city of Dubrovnik.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:49.080
I awoke a short while later, and was informed
by another researcher that I had been administered

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:54.420
with an extremely high dose of amnestics to
negate the effects of SCP-3037.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:58.550
Although later that night, I recalled having
a dream about some delicious Black Risotto

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:00.610
with squid ink sauce.

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:04.819
The Research heads ordered security officers
to gather everyone in the mess hall.

00:16:04.819 --> 00:16:10.190
The Foundation was seemingly aware that they
had an imposter, a charlatan, in their midst

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.520
pretending to be a member of personnel.

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:16.449
I made sure my facial expression did not betray
that I was the one they sought.

00:16:16.449 --> 00:16:20.870
Not one of these philistines could appreciate
my goal of infiltrating Site-19.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:25.769
Not one had the slightest clue how revolutionary
my actions would be for the art world.

00:16:25.769 --> 00:16:30.730
However, although staying vigilant, I became
increasingly aware time might not be on my

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.690
side much longer.

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:38.240
After overhearing a conversation about something
known as SCP-035, I began conducting my own

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:40.600
secretive research into what it was.

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:44.940
I discovered information on the so-called
‘Possessive Mask’ within the Foundation’s

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:45.940
archives.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:51.000
Immediately, my heart skipped a beat, the
porcelain mask that shifted from an exaggerated

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:56.170
comedic grin to a wide frown; it would have
made for a perfect art piece to present to

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.120
AWCY.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:03.990
Much to my annoyance, SCP-035 wasn’t housed
at Site-19, however.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:07.829
Over the next few days, I petitioned to have
the Possessive Mask transferred to the facility

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:09.860
so I could claim it.

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:12.770
My greatest anomalous art piece stayed out
of reach though.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:17.270
I was informed by senior research staff that
the mask posed a potential danger; anyone

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:22.189
nearby was compelled to wear it and would
thus be taken over by its influence.

00:17:22.189 --> 00:17:26.309
Additionally, questions started to be raised
as to why I was requesting this transfer in

00:17:26.309 --> 00:17:27.439
the first place.

00:17:27.439 --> 00:17:30.549
I was starting to grow concerned that my cover
had been blown.

00:17:30.549 --> 00:17:33.520
By now, it very much seemed that my time was
running out.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:38.809
And although it shames me to admit, to say
I handled that notion with grace and professionality…

00:17:38.809 --> 00:17:41.820
well, that would be a lie.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:45.330
Suddenly, I found it: the perfect anomalous
art piece.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:49.340
While rummaging around the Foundation’s
files, I located one about something known

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:52.169
as SCP-1379.

00:17:52.169 --> 00:17:56.700
As I read more, I realized it would secure
my spot within AWCY and forever grant me the

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.610
recognition I deserved.

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:01.270
Even better: it was a painting!

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:04.940
Such a classical art medium, they’d never
be able to refuse me!

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:10.820
As I looked at it directly for the first time,
I knew I had found my masterpiece.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:14.720
SCP-1379 depicted a clown and two children
on the canvas.

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:19.970
But the subject of the painting would hardly
matter to AWCY, they only cared that the artwork

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:21.679
was anomalous.

00:18:21.679 --> 00:18:25.210
And SCP-1379 certainly was.

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:29.940
This painting would allow me to exact revenge
on the AWCY members who’d failed to recognize

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:33.669
my artistic genius for so long.

00:18:33.669 --> 00:18:39.710
Anyone that made a critical remark on SCP-1379
– which those AWCY snobs were bound to do

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:43.559
when they saw it – would first experience
intense pain.

00:18:43.559 --> 00:18:47.340
After twenty minutes of sharp, severe agony,
the sensations would subside.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.240
But that’d only be the start of it.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:56.630
Everyone at the AWCY that dared to criticize
my Magnum Opus, my SCP-1379, would rapidly

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:58.940
lose their ability to imagine.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:02.880
Any of their creativity that had allowed them
to laud their artistic superiority over me

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.060
would cease to exist.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:10.289
None of them would ever make anomalous art
again, and they’d surely be forced to finally

00:19:10.289 --> 00:19:15.320
accept that I am, in fact, the greatest artist
of all time.

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:20.890
From the moment I saw it, I began to plan
my escape, with SCP-1379 in tow.

00:19:20.890 --> 00:19:26.720
A hundred days have passed since I arrived
here, and when I leave, it will be with SCP-1379

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:27.720
and this journal.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:32.330
I’ve not only chronicled how I infiltrated
the SCP Foundation, but also cataloged more

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:35.910
about anomalous art than anyone at AWCY ever
has.

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:40.409
Once I’ve lifted that painting from its
containment locker, my old friends at the

00:19:40.409 --> 00:19:44.610
Insurgency have arranged for me to be extracted
from Site-19.

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:49.990
With SCP-1379, finally I’ll achieve my dream
of-

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:55.450
This journal – belonging to one Stathis
Sterns – has been seized by the SCP Foundation.

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:59.890
Sterns was discovered to have infiltrated
Site-19 after only a handful of days.

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:04.539
However, given that he posed little threat,
and never left the facility, security officers

00:20:04.539 --> 00:20:09.010
were instructed to monitor him closely until
he attempted to escape.

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:14.000
Aware of intelligence regarding various SCPs
noted within his journal, the Foundation deemed

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:18.140
Stathis Sterns a security risk after one-hundred
days.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:22.630
Subject was arrested attempting to escape
and is to be administered amnestics.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:26.700
His journal will remain at Site-19 until further
notice.

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:31.550
Now go and check out “I Survived 100 Days
of SCP-001 When Day Breaks...

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:35.730
Here's What Happened” and “I Survived
100 Days in SCP-3008...

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:39.519
Here's What Happened” for more thrilling
100 day survival challenges!

